  "query_preview": "..\n\nMoist low level easterly flow entrenched locally late this \nafternoon, maintained within the outer influence of the remnant \ncirculation of post-tropical cyclone Isaias now ejecting northeast \nacross the southern Ohio valley. Any lingering pockets of light \nshowers/drizzle will end as meaningful mid level deformation \nassociated with this system vacates and moisture depth diminishes, \nleaving simply extensive stratus heading into the evening period. \nLow to mid level ridging will then build into the region through the \nnight. Combination of increasing magnitude of dry air advection and \ngeneral subsidence will support a clearing sky from northwest to \nsoutheast. Favorable environment will then emerge for some degree of \nfog development late tonight. Forecast will continue to highlight \npatchy mention, but a more focused region could materialize should \nwinds fully decouple. Overnight low temperatures will remain mild \nfor mid-October, bottoming out in the Low to mid 50s across most \nlocations, with upper 50s held in the Metro Detroit urban corridor. \n\nHigher magnitude upper ridging briefly takes residence during the \nearly week period. This occurs as departing surface ridging affords \na shift toward low level southwest flow, offering a period of strong \nwarm air advection starting Monday. A moderately mixed profile under \nhigh insolation potential as 850 mb temperatures push into the mid \nteens will translate into well above normal temperatures. Highs \nMonday arriving in the upper 70s to lower 80s most locations. Very \nmild conditions persist into Monday night with temps holding in the \n60s, as a frontal boundary eases southward into the northern great \nlakes. Meaningful nocturnal window for moist isentropic ascent will \nbring potential for some shower production during the overnight \nhours. Mid level instability increases during this time, supportive \nof an isolated thunder mention. Temperatures will remain a solid 10-\n15 degrees above average Tuesday as 850 mb temps peak near 18c ahead \nof an inbound cold front. Weak cold front slips through late \nTuesday, which could offer a lower end probability for shower \ndevelopment although greater ascent will remain positioned to the \nnorth. \n\nShortwave energy working across the lingering elevated frontal slope \nwill bring another opportunity for light rain development Tuesday \nnight into early Wednesday, with greater potential with southward \nextent. Otherwise, pattern transitions back to more seasonable \nconditions for the latter half of the work week as transient low to \nmid level ridging lifts through. Potentially unsettled conditions \nThursday night into Friday as general split flow yields an influx of \nmoisture from the south with the possibility of interaction with a \nmore dynamic wave ejecting southeast. This northern stream wave will \nprovide a more noteworthy downward adjustment in temperature for \nnext weekend. Highs likely parked in the 50s, with lows pushing well \ninto the 30s.",
